A||N||9||DaveL||Headlight Advics||||6||
Z||000000||DaveL||10-25-2000||09:39 AM||||I noticed the other day that my headlights weren't going up evenly, one slightly before the other. When I checked the motors and things, I found some real wear on the plastic surfaces that slide against each other during raising and lowering. I put some gorrila snot on both places on the headlight doors, and the 2 places on top of the headlight assemblies that slide against the lid, and the problem disappeared! It struck me that continuously monitoring this could extend the life of these expensive motors!<BR>What is the best lube to use in a case like this?||||reg||6
Z||000001||2xsess||10-25-2000||10:52 AM||||What is gorilla snot?<P>I've noticed the same thing about the friction areas.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||11:17 AM||||I use a lube called <B>Break-Free CLP</B>. It was developed for the military. I used it in the national guard. It was the best stuff I ever used. It<B> <BR>cleans <BR>Lubricates <BR>Protects</B> <BR>all parts. It is super slippery. We used to spray it right into the weapons and it would Fire perfect with no jams and would run great. I got some at home I Found at wal-mart. It can also be bought online at different places. I just searched and found it here <A HREF="http://www.milarm.com/linkpage/breakfre/breakfre.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.milarm.com/linkpage/breakfre/breakfre.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000003||StuGood||10-25-2000||01:00 PM||||Great tip!  I have noticed how "groovy" the friction surfaces on the undersides of the headlight doors get, too, and wondered what to do about it.  Even thought about putting a couple rollers on the little rounded "ears" of the headlight frame.<P>Main goal for me, is to improve the headlight actuator's response time.  Hate it when I want to flash my headlights to greet another Fiero, but the lights don't come up in time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> .  <P>I'm going to try the Break-Free CLP stuff if I can find it.  If it helps the headlights come up quicker, I'll be one happy camper   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks again   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ! <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||10-25-2000||01:31 PM||||hmm, I would think CLP would dry up pretty fast. and I'd be a little parinoid about using it clse to any painted surfaces. We use CLP to lube the weapons yes, but we also use it to break up carbon deposites inside the barrel and chamber. If it works tho, I'd love to know. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||05:32 PM||||I should explain how I lube better. I forgot part. I put a thick grease on the plastic surfaces that slide against each other & the clp on all the other parts of the motor system. I used the grease I got from the Fiero store that is used for poly bushings. It is really thick and gooey. My motors seem to run so fast and quiet now.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||10-25-2000||08:16 PM||||I use Sil-glyde (sp?) lubricant stick.  It does seem to help.<P>If it's not raining, I'll usually open the hood before turning-on the headlights.  It looks dorky, but takes a load off the headlight motors.  Might wear-out my hood release, though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-25-2000||08:28 PM||||Any kind of light duty grease that won't run or drip should work for the pivots.<P>If you look at the doors there are actually skid plates on the doors where the light frame hits it. Wouldn't worry much about them.<P>I mostly avoid grease in this type application. Grease tends to hold dirt which can work into the pivots. Instead I use a teflon loaded oil, usually a spray one. The oil will wash away most dirt and leave a film of teflon and oil behind. Being a thin film it doesn't tend to hold as much crud as grease.<P>Unless something is wrong with the lights they are going about as fast as they ar going to go. They weren't made for flashing. What I wish I could do was a useful daytime running light. Someone around had an article for doing it thru the turn signals but I don't like that myself. Maybe I'll look into using a set of driving lights.<P>BTW.... GM does have a kit for enabling DRL on cars with non covered fixed headlights. You can get it from the GM dealer network. (I'm thinking about using this kit with driving lights. All depends what it costs.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaveL||10-26-2000||03:54 PM||||"Gorilla Snot" is a very slippery, extremely slimy substance found usually on the upper lips of rather large primates, and in some cases, Autozone...||||reg||1
Z||000009||webbee||10-26-2000||07:27 PM||||We always called that yellow trim adhesive, gorilla snot. Guess it depends on where you are, what things are called. Thanks for the clarifacation. ||||reg||1
